//RS 
//RS /// I1's one exception: the examples that TEACH the audited `#Unsafe` tier, so
//RS /// their generated Rust must carry `unsafe` — in gated `unsafe { … }` /
//RS /// `unsafe fn` / `unsafe extern` form only. Every other stem must generate none.
//RS ///
//RS /// DERIVED, never hand-typed: the repository already keeps exactly one registry
//RS /// of approved user-written unsafe regions — the `unsafe-ratchet` baseline in
//RS /// `Docs/spec/safety.md`, scanned and enforced by
//RS /// `Tools/agent/check-unsafe-ratchet.mjs` (`tests/unsafe_ratchet.rs`,
//RS /// `Tools/agent/verify-full.sh`). A new `#Unsafe` gate cannot land without
//RS /// refreshing that baseline in the same change, so reading it here makes the two
//RS /// facts one fact.
//RS ///
//RS /// A hand copy was a second registry of the same fact, and it rotted twice: the
//RS /// dev-sentry/gate-ladder rows were red from 2026-08-13 with nothing watching
//RS /// `golden`, and `a5bea5f25` added an approved gate at
//RS /// `Examples/features/crypto/random_api_split.jet:28` ("compare the typed and
//RS /// raw HKDF rungs"), refreshed the baseline as the ratchet demands, and left the
//RS /// list behind — so `golden` reported an APPROVED region as an I1 violation.
//RS ///
//RS /// Deriving cannot widen I1: a region only enters the baseline through the
//RS /// ratchet, a stem with no approved region that emits `unsafe` still fails the
//RS /// negative assertion below, and a stem with one that stops emitting still fails
//RS /// the positive one.
//RS ///
//RS /// Both directions are still asserted below, and the gated form is scanned with
//RS /// `common::unsafe_keyword_columns` — three audited stems carry the word in
//RS /// their own file name, which a plain substring scan could not tell from code.
//RS static GATED_UNSAFE_STEMS: std::sync::LazyLock<std::collections::HashSet<String>> =
//RS     std::sync::LazyLock::new(|| {
//RS         let baseline_path = PathBuf::from(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")).join("Docs/spec/safety.md");
